cale":"medium","map_unit_name":"Lansing Group","strat_name":"Lansing Group","age":"Missourian (Pennsylvanian)","lith":"limestone, shale","descrip":"The Lansing consists of two limestone formations and an intervening shale formation. The Lansing forms an escarpment traced readily across eastern Kansas. In the southern part of the outcrop, limestone units thicken locally into limestone mounds or banks. The overlying shales generally thin over the limestone mounds and thicken where the limestone units thin. Average thickness of the Lansing is about 85 ft (26 m).","comments":null,"t_age":303.7,"b_age":304.1313,"b_interval":982,"t_interval":982,"strat_name_id":[2528],"unit_id":[3965,3966,3967,4462,4463,4464],"lith_classes":["sedimentary"],"lith_types":["carbonate","siliciclastic"],"lith_id":[8,30],"color":"#a1aca8","area":null,"tiny_area":null,"small_area":null,"medium_area":null,"large_area":null},{"legend_id":120523,"source_id":447,"scale":"medium","map_unit_name":"Kansas City Group","strat_name":"Kansas City Group","age":"Upper Pennsylvanian","lith":"coal, limestone, sandstone, shale","descrip":"The Kansas City Group is predominantly made up of alternating limestone and shale beds with minor amounts of coal, underclay, and sandstone","comments":null,"t_age":304.045,"b_age":305.8357,"b_interval":982,"t_interval":982,"strat_name_id":[2518],"unit_id":[3377,3378,3379,3380,3381,3382,3383,3384,3385,3386,3387,3388,3413,3969,3970,3971,3972,3973,3974,3975,3976,3977,3978,3979,4102,4103,4104,4106,4108,4109,4111,4112,4113,4114,4115,42143],"lith_classes":["sedimentary"],"lith_types":["carbonate","siliciclastic","organic"],"lith_id":[8,10,30,38],"color":"#e4e0e3","area":null,"tiny_area":null,"small_area":null,"medium_area":null,"large_area":null},{"legend_id":125562,"source_id":495,"scale":"medium","map_unit_name":"Kansas City Group","strat_name":"Kansas City Group","age":"Missourian (Pennsylvanian)","lith":null,"descrip":"The Kansas City Group contains both marine and nonmarine limestones, shales, and sandstones. Its thickness ranges from about 325 ft (99 m) near Iola to about 350 ft (107 m ) in the Kansas City area. Many of the limestones are cross bedded, oolitic, and algal and locally thicken to form marine mounds or banks similar to those in the Lansing Group.","comments":null,"t_age":304.0833,"b_age":305.8357,"b_interval":982,"t_interval":982,"strat_name_id":[2518],"unit_id":[3969,3970,3971,3972,3973,3974,3975,3976,3977,3978,3979,4466,4467,4468,4469,4470,4471,4472,4473,4474,4475,42143],"lith_classes":["sedimentary"],"lith_types":["carbonate","siliciclastic"],"lith_id":[8,10,30],"color":"#e4e0e3","area":null,"tiny_area":null,"small_area":null,"medium_area":null,"large_area":null},{"legend_id":120534,"source_id":447,"scale":"medium","map_unit_name":"Pleasanton Group","strat_name":"Pleasanton Group","age":"Upper Pennsylvanian","lith":"clastic","descrip":"The Pleasanton Group is predominantly clastic, composed of shales and sandstones. The sandstones are typically medium-grained and micaceous and are often identifiable as channel-fill deposits","comments":null,"t_age":305.6167,"b_age":306.0,"b_interval":982,"t_interval":982,"strat_name_id":[2614],"unit_id":[3390,3414,4118,4119,4120],"lith_classes":["sedimentary"],"lith_types":["siliciclastic"],"lith_id":[8,10],"color":"#c3f0d4","area":null,"tiny_area":null,"small_area":null,"medium_area":null,"large_area":null},{"legend_id":120529,"source_id":447,"scale":"medium","map_unit_name":"Marmaton Group","strat_name":"Marmaton Group","age":"Middle Pennsylvanian","lith":"clay, coal, limestone, shale","descrip":"The Marmaton Group consists of a succession of shale, limestone, clay, and coal beds with some sandstones. It contains more limestone units than the underlying Cherokee Group, which are also thicker and more persistent","comments":null,"t_age":306.8572,"b_age":311.5,"b_interval":983,"t_interval":983,"strat_name_id":[2541],"unit_id":[3417,3418,3419,3420,3421,3495,3496,3497,3498,3499,3500,3501,3